# PHASE 15 — AI/ML ANALYSIS

## Where AI Is Genuinely Required

| Component | Why AI | Alternative Without AI |
|---|---|---|
| **Silage color quality scoring** | Humans disagree 40% of the time on visual grading. CNN provides consistent, quantifiable scores | Manual color chart comparison — inconsistent |
| **Mold/fungal detection** | Identifying early-stage mold colonies (<5mm) requires trained pattern recognition | Naked eye misses early mold in 60% of cases |
| **pH strip colorimetry** | HSV extraction with ML compensation for lighting variation outperforms raw threshold comparison | Manual comparison with printed chart — ±0.5 pH error |

## Where AI Is NOT Needed (Traditional Algorithms Are Better)

| Component | Why NOT AI |
|---|---|
| **BIS compliance mapping** | Deterministic rule engine — thresholds are defined by regulation |
| **AFM1 carry-over projection** | Mathematical formula (exponential model from published research) |
| **Remediation advisory** | Decision tree based on grade + specific failure mode |
| **QR code generation** | Standard encoding algorithm |
| **Data sync** | Standard REST API + conflict resolution |

## Model Architecture

### Model 1: Silage Visual Quality Classifier
- **Architecture:** MobileNetV2 (transfer learning from ImageNet)
- **Input:** 224×224 RGB image (center-cropped, white-balanced)
- **Output:** 4 classes — Excellent (olive-green), Good (yellow-green), Poor (brown), Spoiled (black/moldy)
- **Training data:** ~2,000 images (1,000 real + 1,000 augmented) — sources: Roboflow, custom collection, agricultural extension photos
- **Quantization:** INT8 post-training quantization
- **Model size:** ~3 MB (.tflite)
- **Inference time:** <100ms on mid-range Android

### Model 2: Mold Patch Detector
- **Architecture:** EfficientDet-Lite0 (object detection)
- **Input:** Full-resolution image
- **Output:** Bounding boxes + classes (Aspergillus, Fusarium, Penicillium, Mucor, Clean)
- **Training data:** ~1,500 annotated images from food spoilage datasets + augmentation
- **Model size:** ~5 MB (.tflite)
- **Inference time:** <200ms

### Model 3: pH Strip Reader
- **Architecture:** NOT a neural network — deterministic HSV colorimetry algorithm
- **Steps:**
  1. Detect strip region using contour detection
  2. Identify color pads using template matching
  3. Sample RGB values from each pad center
  4. Convert to HSV color space
  5. Apply von Kries chromatic adaptation using reference card
  6. Map calibrated hue values to pH using polynomial regression (trained on 500 strip images)
- **Accuracy:** ±0.15 pH units (validated in research)

## Explainability
Every result shows exactly WHY the grade was assigned:
```
GRADE: MARGINAL ⚠️
├── Visual Score: 62/100 (Brown patches detected → Maillard browning)
├── pH: 4.6 (Above optimal 4.2 threshold — aerobic exposure)
├── Mold: None detected ✓
├── Moisture: HIGH (ball holds shape too firmly)
└── BIS IS 19562:2026: Fails Clause 5.2.1 (pH > 4.5)
```

## Model Drift & Continuous Improvement
- Crowdsourced validation: when connectivity available, images and grades are synced
- Cooperative quality officers can flag incorrect grades → feeds into retraining pipeline
- Quarterly model updates pushed via app update

